Revision history for DBIx-Squirrel

Unreleased

v2.0.5 2026-11-08

    The successor to 1.6.5: a rewrite in modern Perl.

    The reference and the how-to ship as POD manuals, so MetaCPAN and
    perldoc have them with the modules (roadmap 2026-0001).

    Code written for 1.x needs changes to run. "Upgrading from 1.x", in
    DBIx::Squirrel::Manual::Reference, lists them. To stay on 1.x, which
    supports older Perls, install its last version:
    cpanm DBIx::Squirrel@1.6.5. Its code is archived at
    https://github.com/nukopian/DBIx-Squirrel-1.

    - Requires Perl 5.36 or later. Compress::Bzip2, Crypt::CBC, Dotenv,
      JSON::Syck and YAML::Syck are no longer dependencies, and loading
      the module no longer reads a .env file.
    - Placeholders in the styles ?, ?1, :1, $1, :name and $name, one
      style to a statement, each rewritten as ? for the DBI. Quoted
      strings and identifiers, comments, casts and PostgreSQL's
      dollar-quoted bodies are left alone, read by the database's rules;
      the ekorn_dialect attribute names the database behind DBD::ODBC
      or DBD::Proxy. DBD::Pg's placeholder options are followed.
      Comments are passed on to the database, where 1.x removed them.
    - A numbered or named placeholder takes one value wherever it
      appears. Numbers are written in the digits 0-9: a digit of another
      script is part of a name, there and in get_column, pluck and
      bind_param, rather than read as 0. Named values come as pairs or
      in a hash reference, with or without the sigil, and an array
      reference stands for the values in it. Values that don't match
      the placeholders croak, and so does executing a statement with
      some left unbound.
    - A placeholder with "..." before it, such as ...:ids or ...?, takes
      a list, written as a ? for each of its values, for IN and anywhere
      else SQL takes a list. The statement is prepared with its values,
      by do, the select methods, iterate, results and Sugar's functions;
      prepare and prepare_cached croak given one, and so do an empty
      list and a list holding a reference. To a statement whose one
      value is a list, a single array reference is the list. An
      iterator's reset prepares the statement again for a list of
      another length.
    - A statement can be a string, lines in an array reference, a code
      reference returning either, or a statement handle.
    - do and the select methods take bind values without the
      attributes in front. do returns what the DBI's do returns, in any
      context: 1.x also returned the statement handle in list context.
    - connect and prepare croak if they fail, whatever RaiseError says.
      connect clones a database handle given in place of a data source.
    - Errors, and the DBI's warnings, are reported at the line that
      called DBIx::Squirrel, naming the method called, as plain DBI
      reports them.
    - iterate returns a DBIx::Squirrel::Iterator, replacing
      DBIx::Squirrel::it, with next, all, single, reset, count, column_names and
      sth, and no aliases. next no longer sets $_, and single croaks on a
      second result, where 1.x warned. Rows are fetched 100 at a time,
      in the shape the ekorn_slice attribute gives, replacing slice,
      cache_size and their defaults; all, without stages, fetches the
      rest at once, close to the DBI's own speed. column_names gives the
      statement's column names, read when it's executed, as DBD::mysql
      gives none once the last row is fetched.
    - Stages, 1.x's transformations, follow the bind values. Each takes
      a value as its argument and in $_, and returns nothing to drop it,
      one value, or several. Stop, from DBIx::Squirrel::Stop, ends a
      pipeline early, and DBIx::Squirrel::Context gives a stage its
      iterator, result_number and result_original.
    - DBIx::Squirrel::Pipeline runs any values through stages, and pipe
      and tap run a database or statement handle through them.
      A pipeline's stages method returns its stages, to give wherever
      stages go, with more after them.
    - Once a stage dies, an iterator or result set has ended: next and
      all return nothing more, and the statement is finished; reset
      runs it again from the start.
    - gather, from DBIx::Squirrel::Gather, makes a stage afresh for each
      run of its pipeline, from a block returning the stage and,
      optionally, a function called once the values run out, whose
      values go through the rest of the stages. A stage can batch rows,
      make one value from all of them, or keep a count that starts
      again on each execution.
    - Canned stages, each module exporting its usual ones:
      DBIx::Squirrel::Stages::Flow's take and drop, with take_while and
      drop_while on request; DBIx::Squirrel::Stages::Rows's pluck and
      as_hash, for rows of any shape; DBIx::Squirrel::Stages::Batch's
      batch and collect, passing values on in batches, or all at once;
      and DBIx::Squirrel::Stages::Taps's peek and count_into, with
      dump_to on request, looking at values as they go by.
    - The pipeline is Ekorn::Pipeline, a distribution of its own, which
      began here, and which DBIx::Squirrel requires, from v0.1.1 up to,
      but not including, v0.2.0. DBIx::Squirrel::Pipeline, Stop, Gather
      and Pipes, and the stages in DBIx::Squirrel::Stages::Flow, Batch
      and Taps, are DBIx::Squirrel's names for its modules, and stay:
      each is a subclass of Ekorn's, or exports Ekorn's functions, from
      a list of its own. A stage that catches errors throws a stop on if
      it isa Ekorn::Pipeline::Stop, which every stop is, including those
      the canned stages throw.
    - results returns a DBIx::Squirrel::Results, replacing
      DBIx::Squirrel::rs, whose rows are DBIx::Squirrel::Result objects,
      replacing DBIx::Squirrel::rc: one class for each list of column
      names, with a method for each column, in lower case. get_column
      takes a name or a position, and the ekorn_result_class attribute
      sets the rows' base class.
    - DBIx::Squirrel::Sugar replaces database_entities: a function for
      each database, connected when it's first used, and for each
      statement, with stages of its own.
    - prepare_cached keeps apart statements rewritten the same, such as
      SELECT :a, :b and SELECT :b, :a, and never hands out a handle an
      iterator is part way through.
    - The ekorn_finish_active attribute finishes a statement still
      active before executing it again.
    - Attributes starting ekorn_ aren't passed on to the DBI. Given to
      connect, they're the defaults for every statement the handle
      prepares.
    - use DBIx::Squirrel takes the names the DBI exports, such as
      :sql_types, as use DBI does.
    - The reference and the how-to ship as POD,
      DBIx::Squirrel::Manual::Reference and DBIx::Squirrel::Manual::HowTo,
      made from their Markdown, so MetaCPAN and perldoc have them with
      the modules (roadmap 2026-0001).
    - SECURITY.md says how to report a vulnerability, privately, and
      CONTRIBUTING.md how to report a bug or ask for a feature; the
      distribution takes issues, not pull requests.
    - Gone, as out of scope: DBIx::Squirrel::Crypt::Fernet, the
      DBIx::Squirrel::Transform modules for JSON and YAML, load_tuples,
      and the file and encoding helpers in DBIx::Squirrel::util. Those
      for Data::Dumper and IO give way to dump_to and peek.
